module literal_arm.neg_1

// A LITERAL BELOW THE ROOT of a `match` arm's pattern -- READ, JUDGED, AND
// REFUSED BY NAME.
//
// THIS CELL HAS CHANGED SIDES TWICE, AND WHAT IT GUARDS NEVER CHANGED: the
// order of preference -- a named refusal is louder than a crash and quieter
// than a wrong answer.
//
// 2026-09-20: positive -> negative. The parser had learnt string and char arm
// patterns, and novac DIED on `"match" => 2` in the emitter (`match pattern is
// neither an int literal nor an identifier`); the cell held the refusal that
// replaced the crash.
//
// 2026-10-01: the root string arm compiles (match_str_lit/pos_1 holds it), and
// the cell moves to the next hole of the same family. A literal INSIDE a
// pattern -- `W("match")` -- is legal Nova (D486 s2), but the lowering tests
// the arm's ROOT only: for an int the tag of `A(3)` is tested and the `3`
// dropped, so `A(4)` takes the arm, measured on the day the string arms
// opened. The root's door would have handed strings the same silent wrong
// answer; the int half is a defect of its own, reported beside this cell.

// NOVAC_EXPECT a string or char literal inside a `match` arm's pattern

type Word enum
    | W(str)
    | Other

fn word_kind(w Word) -> int => match w {
    W("match") => 2
    _ => 0
}
